## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests incremental geometry design, amortized analysis under streaming batch updates, and practical data structure trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic convex hull maintenance with batch insertions
- Amortized cost analysis as n = |P| grows with varying B
- Hull merging via common tangents and angular ordering
- Query-time guarantees for inside-hull membership tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Merge two convex hulls H and B (both in clockwise order)
function mergeConvexHulls(H, B) {
  if (B.length === 0) return H;
  if (H.length === 0) return B;

  // Find lower and upper common tangents between H and B
  const { lowerTan, upperTan } = findCommonTangents(H, B);

  // Rebuild the affected portion between tangent vertices
  const merged = rebuildHullSegment(H, B, lowerTan, upperTan);
  return merged;
}
```

## Two Practical Optimizations
1. **Batch Coalescing**: Accumulate multiple batches in W before merging. This reduces merge frequency when B is small relative to h, yielding better amortized cost.
2. **Hull Caching**: Store precomputed angular ranges for each hull vertex to accelerate binary search queries, trading space for faster lookups.

## Performance Comparison: Incremental Merge vs. Rebuilding
| Approach | Per-Batch Cost | Query Time |
|----------|----------------|------------|
| Incremental merge | O(B + h) amortized | O(log h) |
| Full rebuild | O((n) log n) | O(log n) |

As n grows, incremental merging becomes increasingly advantageous since rebuilding re-sorts all n points each batch, while the incremental approach processes only O(B) new points plus O(h) for the affected hull region.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about I/O efficiency, batching, and dynamic graph algorithms under memory constraints; blends theory with practical concerns like latency and cache locality.

## Key Concepts

- External memory I/O model (M, B) and batch updates
- Dynamic connectivity with batched insertions
- Amortized analysis and rebuild strategies
- Spanning forests and residual edges
- Locality-aware data partitioning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: batch I/O efficient dynamic connectivity
class DynConn {
  constructor(T){ this.F = new Forest(); this.buffer = [];
  addBatch(edges){
    this.buffer.push(edges);
    if(this.buffer.length * b >= T) this.rebuild();
  }
  connected(u,v){
    return this.F.find(u) === this.F.find(v) || 
           this.searchResidual(u,v);
  }
  rebuild(){
    // merge buffered edges into compact structure
    // amortized O((n+mb)/B) I/Os
  }
}
```

## Performance Analysis

**(i) Data Structure:** Maintain spanning forest in RAM, buffered edges on disk with locality-aware partitioning, and periodic rebuild of compact connectivity structure.

**(ii) Amortized I/O:** O((n + mb)/B) per batch, where rebuild occurs every T/b batches.

**(iii) Query Time:** O(log_B n) average case, O(m/B) worst case when searching residual edges.

**(iv) Space:** O(n + m + T) where T is buffer threshold, typically Θ(n + m).

**(v) Optimizations:** 1) Adaptive rebuild threshold based on update/query ratio; 2) Multi-level buffering with hot/cold edge classification.

**(vi) Comparison:** Recomputing from scratch costs O((n+m)α(n,m)/B) per batch vs. O((n+mb)/B) amortized with batching—significant improvement when b << m.

